  "query_preview": "..\nSunday through Wednesday: The upper level that was offshore finally\nmoves into northern California tonight, bringing clouds and \nlight rain to southeast Washington and the southern Idaho \nPanhandle. Because the best dynamics are shifting south, the \nchance for substantial rain tonight is decreasing. There is a \n10-30% chance of 0.10\u201d or more of rain through Sunday afternoon \nfor the Palouse, foothills of the Blue Mountains, L-C valley, \nand Camas Prairie. Dry air filters in from the west during the \nday behind the base of the shortwave trough, which will lead to \nvery sunny skies filtering into most of the region except \nlingering low stratus in southeast Washington and the central \nand southern ID Panhandle. There will be a risk for fog Sunday \nnight with clear skies across the region. Forecasting fog is a \nlow confidence endeavor but high resolution models are hinting \nat fog development on the northern Palouse and chances \nincreasing to the north and west overnight. Monday through \nWednesday afternoon remains quiet and dry with near normal \ntemperatures in the upper 40s to mid 50s. \n\nWednesday night through Sunday: A couple of waves will move\nthrough the region mid to late week, bringing the potential for\nrain and mountain snow. From 4 AM Thursday to 4 AM Saturday, \nthere is a 5-10% chance of 4+ inches of snow for Stevens Pass \nand Lookout Pass and a 30% chance for Washington Pass. The Gulf \nof Alaska longwave trough remains firmly anchored into next \nweekend with shortwaves pivoting around it, favoring ridging \nbuilding into our area which would favor drier conditions in 70%\nof the global ensemble. This does not mean it would be \ncompletely dry, especially the Cascades. 30% of the global \nensemble resembles a dirty ridge with deeper troughing in the \nnortheast Pacific and displaced further east towards the Pacific\nnorthwest. Overall, the next week is drier than we would like \nto see for the start of November. /DB",
